mcprack for Debian
===================

## Installation

Run the package installer, which will prompt for configuration interactively:

    sudo dpkg -i mcprack_*.deb

The installer will ask for:
- Database engine (sqlite3, PostgreSQL, or MySQL) and connection details,
  via the standard `dbconfig-common` prompts. sqlite3 is the default and
  needs no server; choosing pgsql/mysql has dbconfig-common create the
  database and role for you. Re-run `dpkg-reconfigure mcprack` later to
  change engine/connection details.
- Vaultwarden (Bitwarden) server URL, client ID, client secret, master password
- Whether to enable LDAP authentication (optional, disabled by default)
- If LDAP enabled: server URL, base DN, bind DN, bind password

All configuration is saved to `/etc/mcprack/env`. Database migrations
(`flask db upgrade`, via Alembic) run automatically on every install and
upgrade. If no admin account exists yet, an `admin` account with a randomly
generated password is created automatically — the password is written to
`/etc/mcprack/admin-credentials` (root-only, mode 0600) and printed once
during an interactive install.

## Manual Configuration (if not using interactive install)

Configuration lives in `/etc/mcprack/env`. Required variables:

    SECRET_KEY=<random-string>
    SQLALCHEMY_DATABASE_URI=sqlite:////var/lib/mcprack/mcprack.db
    # or postgresql+psycopg2://... / mysql+pymysql://... (install python3-psycopg2
    # or python3-pymysql respectively — see Suggests in debian/control)
    #
    # Normally you don't set this by hand — `dpkg-reconfigure mcprack` drives
    # dbconfig-common to pick the engine and (re)write this value.

### Demo mode (optional, off by default)

    DEMO_MODE=true

For a public-facing instance: blocks registering/editing/deleting MCP servers
under Admin -> Servers (a server's `command` runs as-is whenever any user
connects to it, so this isn't something a public admin account should be able
to change), while catalog browsing/selection/config download keep working
normally. Restart the service after changing this.

### Authentication (optional, LDAP disabled by default)

    LDAP_ENABLED=false
    # To enable LDAP:
    LDAP_ENABLED=true
    LDAP_SERVER=ldap://10.11.25.3:389
    LDAP_BASE_DN=dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_USER_OU=ou=Users,dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_USER_FILTER=(sAMAccountName=%(user)s)
    LDAP_BIND_DN=cn=admin,dc=spojent,dc=cz
    LDAP_BIND_PASSWORD=...

### Vaultwarden (Bitwarden) Credentials

    BW_SERVER=https://vaultwarden-dev.proxy.spojenet.cz
    BW_CLIENTID=...
    BW_CLIENTSECRET=...
    BW_PASSWORD=...
    BITWARDENCLI_APPDATA_DIR=/var/lib/mcprack/.bw

Get `BW_CLIENTID`/`BW_CLIENTSECRET` from the Vaultwarden web vault: Account
Settings → Security → Keys (API Key). `BW_PASSWORD` is that same account's
master password. After setting these, log in to mcprack as an admin and open
"Vaultwarden diagnostics" in the nav bar (`/admin/vaultwarden/wizard`) — it
checks the `bw` binary, `BW_SERVER` reachability, the API key, and the master
password one at a time and tells you exactly which one is still wrong,
instead of a raw "bw login failed" error. Re-run it after every edit to
`/etc/mcprack/env`, then restart `mcprack.service` so new values are loaded.

### Manual Setup Steps (if needed)

Migrations and the initial admin account are created automatically by
`postinst` — see above. If you need to redo either by hand (e.g. after
restoring a database dump):

    # Apply migrations
    su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; FLASK_APP=app:create_app python3 -m flask db upgrade'

    # Create another admin account
    su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c 'cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; FLASK_APP=app:create_app python3 -m flask create-admin'

Then start the service:

    systemctl enable --now mcprack.service


## Troubleshooting

### "Permission denied" reading /etc/mcprack/env during postinst

Packages built before 0.2.13 created `/etc/mcprack/env` mode `0600`
(owner-only), so despite the `root:mcprack` group ownership the `mcprack`
system user couldn't actually read it. Postinst's own `flask db upgrade`
and `create-admin` steps run as that user, so they failed silently with:

    sh: 1: .: cannot open /etc/mcprack/env: Permission denied
    ⚠  Database migration failed — check /etc/mcprack/env and run 'flask db upgrade' manually
    ⚠  Failed to create initial admin account — create one manually with 'flask create-admin'

0.2.14 fixes this going forward (permissions are corrected unconditionally
on every postinst run, not just on first install), but an install that hit
this under an older package needs a one-time manual fix:

    sudo chmod 0640 /etc/mcprack/env
    sudo su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c "cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; FLASK_APP=app:create_app python3 -m flask db upgrade"
    sudo su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c "cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; FLASK_APP=app:create_app python3 -m flask create-admin"

### "table already exists" from `flask db upgrade`

If the service ever started successfully *before* migrations ever ran
(e.g. while hitting the permission bug above, on a package older than
0.2.15), `db.create_all()`'s startup fallback already created every table
without recording an Alembic version. A later `flask db upgrade` then
tries to run the very first migration from scratch and fails:

    sqlalchemy.exc.OperationalError: (pymysql.err.OperationalError) (1050, "Table 'mcp_servers' already exists")

0.2.15 stamps Alembic to head right after that fallback runs, so this
shouldn't happen on fresh bootstraps going forward. If you're recovering
an install that already hit it, tell Alembic the schema is current instead
of trying to upgrade it:

    sudo su -s /bin/sh mcprack -c "cd /usr/lib/mcprack && set -a; . /etc/mcprack/env; set +a; FLASK_APP=app:create_app python3 -m flask db stamp head"

## MCP HTTP Proxy (how remote clients reach stdio servers)

Users always connect remotely — mcprack never expects its own web UI and a
user's MCP client to be on the same machine. So a stdio-implemented server
is never handed to a client as a raw local spawn command; the downloaded
config always points at a per-user proxy URL instead
(`/proxy/mcp/<token>/<server_id>`, served by `mcprack.service` itself, no
separate proxy service to install or enable).

The first time a user's client actually connects to that URL, mcprack spawns
a dedicated `fastmcp` subprocess for that (user, server) pair on demand,
resolving that user's credentials (their own override if they've set one,
otherwise the admin's default) at that exact moment — not when the config
was downloaded. Each user gets their own isolated instance of a server, even
if several users have it selected at once. Idle instances (no traffic for 15
minutes) are cleaned up automatically; see Admin → Proxy instances for a live
list, and `python3-fastmcp` must be installed (see Recommends in
`debian/control`) for this to work.


## OpenTelemetry (optional — packaging gap on Debian, read before enabling)

`OTEL_ENABLED=true` in `/etc/mcprack/env` turns on trace/metric export (see
README.md "Observability (OpenTelemetry)"). It is safe to enable even
without every dependency installed — `telemetry.py` catches any import
failure at startup, logs it, and falls back to fully disabled rather than
crashing the service.

As of Debian trixie, only two of the required Python packages are actually
available via `apt`:

    python3-opentelemetry-api
    python3-opentelemetry-sdk
    python3-opentelemetry-instrumentation   (base instrumentation library)

There is **no Debian package** (checked via `apt-cache search opentelemetry`
on trixie) for:

    opentelemetry-instrumentation-flask
    opentelemetry-instrumentation-sqlalchemy
    opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-http
    opentelemetry-exporter-otlp-proto-grpc

Without these four, `OTEL_ENABLED=true` will safely no-op (nothing is
exported, no crash). To actually export anything, either:

- Package those four for Debian (see the `package-python-debian` workflow
  used for other VitexSoftware Python packaging), or
- Install them into a dedicated venv and point mcprack's systemd unit at
  that venv's Python instead of the system interpreter — this deviates
  from mcprack's normal "no virtualenv, system Python packages" packaging
  model (see main README "Packaging"), so treat it as a stopgap, not the
  long-term fix.

Check **Admin → OTEL Diagnostics** (`/admin/otel/wizard`) after enabling —
if `OTEL_ENABLED` shows as *disabled* there even though you set it to
`true` in `/etc/mcprack/env`, initialization failed (missing package) and
the page shows the exact import error under "Last initialization error".
The same error is also in `journalctl -u mcprack.service`.
